<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kufa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kufa was a garrison city founded by Arab Muslim forces in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in the 630s during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> conquests. It became a major political and religious centre and served as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Ali. Its population was central to the conflicts that ended the Rashidun Caliphate, and it remained a centre of Alid sympathy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kufa.jpg" alt="Kufa" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ali sabih kadhim · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%D8%B3%D8%AC%D8%AF_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B3%D9%87%D9%84%D8%A9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This system kept conquered agriculture productive and channelled its surplus into stipends that sustained the Arab military establishment in purpose-built garrison cities, <b>Kufa</b> and Basra in Iraq and Fustat in Egypt, which were at once military camps, administrative centres and growing commercial hubs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>He made Damascus the seat of the caliphate in place of Ali's base at <b>Kufa</b> and the earlier capital of Medina.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The free Muslims of the towns, craftsmen, shopkeepers, porters, water-carriers and labourers, filled the markets of Baghdad, Basra, <b>Kufa</b> and the provincial capitals; Baghdad, founded in 762, was probably the largest city in the world until the 930s.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li><li><blockquote>Wounded when Isa ibn Musa's Abbasid troops scattered his army at Bāchamrā south of <b>Kufa</b>, Ibrahim ibn Abdallah died there, ending the revolt he had led after his brother.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-14">14 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kharijite Ibn Muljam struck the fourth caliph, Ali ibn Abi Talib, with a poisoned sword as he prayed in the Great Mosque of <b>Kufa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-26">26 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His authority depended on governors and tribal allies, and he faced revolts from the Kharijites of the Najdiyya and from al-Mukhtar's pro-Alid movement at <b>Kufa</b>, which Mus'ab defeated in 687.</blockquote><a href="/state/caliphate-of-ibn-al-zubayr">Caliphate of Ibn al-Zubayr</a></li></ul></section>
